  "mode": "ARGUMENT",
  "source_text": "The new authentication service must support OAuth 2.0 and SAML 2.0 protocols. Security audits require multi-factor authentication for all administrative access. If the token refresh interval is set too low, users will experience frequent session timeouts. Because the legacy system uses a monolithic architecture, scaling it horizontally is difficult. The rewrite team recommends a microservices approach, but that argument is flawed because it ignores the complexity of data consistency across services. Traffic analysis shows that 70% of API calls originate from mobile clients. The current rate-limiting strategy is too aggressive and blocks legitimate requests. However, relaxing rate limits without proper throttling could expose the system to DDoS attacks. A token-bucket algorithm with per-client quotas would balance security and usability. Since the compliance deadline is Q3 2026, the team must prioritize authentication features over performance optimizations. This prioritization is misleading because compliance does not guarantee security. In summary, the architecture must evolve while maintaining backward compatibility and without introducing single points of failure.\n",
